{%- comment -%}
  ─── ONE PRODUCT CARD ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  A photograph, a quiet label, and a detail panel that is rendered HERE, on the
  server, hidden, and revealed in place when the shopper taps the photo.

  WHY THE PANEL IS SERVER-RENDERED AND NOT FETCHED. Three reasons, in order of
  how much they matter:

    1. Sold-out sizes. The panel's size row is built from `product.variants`
       and `variant.available` — the SAME two values the product page uses. It
       cannot drift from the product page, cannot be stale, and cannot fail to
       load. A fetched panel would have a state where the sizes have not
       arrived yet, and "no sizes yet" and "no sizes left" look identical on a
       phone. That is the one mistake this site cannot make.
    2. It opens instantly, with no network, so browsing stays browsing.
    3. It works when the endpoint is down, because there is no endpoint.

  The cost is page weight: ~1–15 hidden size buttons per card. At 24 cards a
  page that is a few kilobytes of gzipped markup, which is cheaper than one of
  the photographs.

  WITHOUT JAVASCRIPT the panel simply never un-hides, and the card is a plain
  <a> to the real product page. Nothing is lost but the convenience.

  SEO / SHARING. The photo is wrapped in a real <a href="{{ product.url }}">.
  Crawlers follow it to a real, server-rendered product page — the quick view is
  an enhancement layered on top of a working link, never a replacement for one.
  marathon-storefront.js intercepts the click and pushes that same URL into the
  address bar, so an opened card and a shared link are the same address.

  ACCEPTS
    product      (required)
    lazy         'lazy' | 'eager'   — first row should be eager
    sizes_attr   the responsive `sizes` attribute for this context
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- liquid
  assign lazy = lazy | default: 'lazy'
  assign sizes_attr = sizes_attr | default: '(min-width: 1400px) 18vw, (min-width: 990px) 23vw, (min-width: 600px) 31vw, 47vw'

  # ─── THE CONDITION GRADE ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
  # It is not a metafield. The publishing pipeline writes it into the product
  # description with a fixed template (src/components/shopify/publishShared.js,
  # buildDescriptionHtml) as exactly one paragraph:
  #
  #     <p><strong>Condition:</strong> Excellent — no visible wear</p>
  #
  # so it is read back out of the description here. This is deliberate: adding a
  # metafield would mean a write pass over 3,457 publish records to say something
  # the description already says, and would then have TWO places a grade could
  # live and disagree. Reading the one the shopper is already shown cannot
  # disagree with itself.
  #
  # If the paragraph is ever absent, `condition` stays blank and the panel omits
  # the line rather than inventing a grade — there is no default, by owner spec.
  assign condition = ''
  if product.description contains '<strong>Condition:</strong>'
    assign condition = product.description | split: '<strong>Condition:</strong>' | last | split: '</p>' | first | strip_html | strip
  endif

  assign sold_out = true
  if product.available
    assign sold_out = false
  endif

  # ALT TEXT. Computed here, not inline: a filter written after a named argument
  # of image_tag chains onto image_tag's OWN output, so `alt: x | escape` escapes
  # the whole <img> element and prints it to the shopper as text. (image_tag
  # escapes the alt attribute itself, so no `| escape` is needed or wanted.)
  #
  # The alt is the product's listing title. That title is the one the publishing
  # pipeline already validated against src/utils/shopifyTriggers.js, so it is the
  # compliant string by construction — deriving anything else here would either
  # repeat that validation badly or throw away the only description a screen
  # reader gets.
  assign img_alt = product.featured_media.alt | default: product.title

  # ONE VARIANT = NO PICKER. `has_only_default_variant` is NOT the test: it is
  # true only for a product with no options at all, and every product on this
  # shop carries a real "Size" option — a one-size bag's single variant is
  # titled "One Size", not "Default Title", so has_only_default_variant is false
  # and the shopper would be made to tap a one-item size picker before buying.
  assign single_variant = false
  if product.variants.size == 1
    assign single_variant = true
  endif
-%}

<article
  class="mc-card"
  data-mc-card
  data-mc-url="{{ product.url }}"
  data-mc-title="{{ product.title | escape }}"
>
  <a class="mc-card__media" href="{{ product.url }}" data-mc-open>
    {%- if product.featured_media -%}
      {%- comment -%}
        `featured_media.preview_image`, not `featured_media` — this is exactly
        the path Dawn 15.4.1 uses in its own card snippet. image_url accepts a
        media object in recent API versions, but the preview image is the
        documented, long-standing input and this theme is a Dawn derivative:
        matching it means the image pipeline behaves identically to every other
        image on the shop rather than relying on a newer overload.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      {{
        product.featured_media.preview_image
        | image_url: width: 800
        | image_tag:
          loading: lazy,
          widths: '200,300,400,500,600,700,800',
          sizes: sizes_attr,
          alt: img_alt
      }}
    {%- endif -%}
    {%- if sold_out -%}<span class="mc-card__sold">Sold out</span>{%- endif -%}
  </a>

  <div class="mc-card__meta">
    <a class="mc-card__name" href="{{ product.url }}" data-mc-open>{{ product.title }}</a>
    <span class="mc-card__price{% if sold_out %} mc-card__price--out{% endif %}">
      {{- product.price | money -}}
    </span>
  </div>

  {%- comment -%}
    The panel. `hidden` until JS opens it — and it stays hidden forever if JS
    never runs, which is the graceful degradation described above.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  <div class="mc-panel" hidden data-mc-panel>
    <button type="button" class="mc-panel__close" data-mc-close aria-label="Close">&times;</button>

    <p class="mc-panel__title">{{ product.title }}</p>
    <p class="mc-panel__price">{{ product.price | money }}</p>

    {%- comment -%}
      CONDITION SITS HERE — above the sizes, above the button. It is a public
      claim about used goods and the shopper reads it BEFORE choosing to buy,
      not after. (On the theme's own product template it currently renders
      below Add to cart; see theme/INSTALL.md.)
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {%- if condition != blank -%}
      <p class="mc-panel__condition"><b>Condition:</b> {{ condition }}</p>
    {%- endif -%}

    {%- unless single_variant -%}
      <div>
        <p class="mc-sizes__legend" id="mc-legend-{{ product.id }}">
          {{ product.options_with_values.first.name | default: 'Size' }}
        </p>
        <div class="mc-sizes" role="group" aria-labelledby="mc-legend-{{ product.id }}">
          {%- comment -%}
            EVERY variant is rendered, in the order the product defines them.
            A sold-out size is rendered, is legible, and is `disabled` — it is
            never dropped and never hidden. Dropping it would tell the shopper
            the size was never made; this tells them it is gone.
          {%- endcomment -%}
          {%- for variant in product.variants -%}
            <button
              type="button"
              class="mc-size"
              data-mc-size
              data-variant-id="{{ variant.id }}"
              aria-pressed="false"
              {% unless variant.available %}disabled aria-disabled="true"{% endunless %}
            >{{ variant.title }}{% unless variant.available %}<span class="visually-hidden"> — sold out</span>{% endunless %}</button>
          {%- endfor -%}
        </div>
      </div>
    {%- else -%}
      {%- comment -%} One-size product (bags, fragrance): no picker, but the
        buy button still needs its variant id. {%- endcomment -%}
      <input type="hidden" data-mc-single value="{{ product.selected_or_first_available_variant.id }}">
    {%- endunless -%}

    <div class="mc-panel__actions">
      <p class="mc-panel__status" data-mc-status role="status" aria-live="polite"></p>
      <button
        type="button"
        class="mc-add"
        data-mc-add
        {% if sold_out %}disabled{% endif %}
      >{% if sold_out %}Sold out{% else %}Add to cart{% endif %}</button>
      <a class="mc-panel__link" href="{{ product.url }}">View full details</a>
    </div>
  </div>
</article>
